1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:03,970
Up to four billion people rely
on herbal medicine.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:07,550
Most medicinal plants in trade are
still taken from the wild.

3
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:10,090
And almost no one is
counting what is left.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,220
In the Philippines, about one hundred
seventy-five hectares of tree cover disappear

5
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,270
every day.

6
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,030
But no one measures harvest.

7
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:20,520
So where should a ranger, or a harvester,

8
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:21,450
even start?

9
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:23,850
Take tongkat ali.

10
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,030
Its roots are dug up and sold.

11
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:27,790
It is officially endangered.

12
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:31,630
Yet open databases hold just five
reliable records of it.

13
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:34,970
So we built Harvester Risk Atlas:

14
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:39,300
a free map that ranks which wild medicinal
plants of the Philippines need help

15
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:42,670
first, where they grow, and
how sure we are.

16
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:44,870
It asks three questions.

17
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:46,270
How rare is it?

18
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:47,810
Does harvesting kill it?

19
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,290
Is its home disappearing?

20
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:53,210
Rarity is measured only where
botanists actually searched.

21
00:00:53,860 --> 00:00:56,260
Then the whole ranking runs
two thousand times,

22
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:59,020
with different weights and
different data each time.

23
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,610
So every plant gets a range,
not a single number.

24
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:05,410
Pick tongkat ali on the atlas.

25
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:08,680
You see broad hexagons, never exact spots,

26
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,610
so the plant stays hard to find.

27
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:15,430
Its profile explains why it ranks
second of twenty-nine,

28
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,470
anywhere from first to fifth.

29
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,330
One click gives a ranger a field brief.

30
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:25,070
Move a slider, and the ranking updates.

31
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:28,570
And now, the year itself.

32
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,660
On the new Seasons page, slide
through the months,

33
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,890
from the dry season into the wet.

34
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:38,850
Watch where a plant is recorded, and when
observers saw it in flower or in fruit,

35
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:41,110
even an invasive weed like makahiya.

36
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:45,270
Plant records pile up from March to
May, when people go out.

37
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:48,570
So every month is weighed against how
many people were looking.

38
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,780
Every name is checked against
World Flora Online,

39
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,750
and every plant against Co's Digital
Flora of the Philippines,

40
00:01:57,060 --> 00:01:59,410
built on the work of botanist Leonardo Co.

41
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:01,950
For tongkat ali, it agrees:

42
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:03,870
native, and endangered.

43
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:08,050
But it also lists Mindoro,
Homonhon, and Bucas Grande,

44
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:10,010
where open data has nothing.

45
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:11,970
That is where to look next.

46
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:17,980
Across all ranked plants, two thirds of the
land next to known records has barely

47
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:18,910
been searched.

48
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:22,170
Five plants need attention now:

49
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:27,210
kalingag, tongkat ali, bitaog,
tibig, and amuyon.

50
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,210
Change the math, and the
order barely moves.

51
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,650
It is not medical advice, and its harvest data
still needs experts in the field.

52
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:38,050
Harvester Risk Atlas.

53
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:42,290
Which plants first, where to look
next, and how sure we are.

54
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,710
Help us protect the plants that heal us.

